================================================================
Reading
================================================================
Acceptance rate decides the sign, and it is not close.

With n_max=8 the cost per step is 8 draft forwards + 1 batched verify, so the
break-even acceptance is roughly 1 - (draft_cost x 8) / (target_cost). The 2B Q8
draft reads ~2.7 GB/token vs the 27B's ~14.6 GB, i.e. roughly 5x cheaper, so
break-even sits around 60-70% acceptance. Measured 24.3% on prose -> a large loss.

100% acceptance on the counting task is not a real workload signal: the draft
predicts a deterministic sequence exactly. Do not use a list/counting prompt to
promote speculation.

================================================================
The real lead: this model ships an MTP head
================================================================
GGUF metadata: qwen35.nextn_predict_layers = 1

So the model has a built-in next-token-prediction head. An MTP head is trained
against the target's own distribution, so its acceptance on real prose should be
far above the 24.3% a foreign 2B draft achieves. llama.cpp has an MTP speculative
type (COMMON_SPECULATIVE_TYPE_DRAFT_MTP, common/common.h:174); 1Cat's own notes
report MTP/4 or DFlash2 lifting SM75-class decode from ~33 to 95-220 tok/s.

This is the untested decode lever, and it uses the model's own head rather than a
mismatched external model.

